[3] Robin/Robbins synthesis for the proof target
  Robin's number-theory criterion is a scalar extremal inequality for
  sigma(n)/n; it is useful here as a warning that divisor averages do not
  remember phase labels by themselves.
  Robbins' graph theorem is the better proof analogy: strong orientation
  is possible exactly when the graph has no bridge.  For LRC14, the
  certificate graph should be orientable only after packet labels retain
  the load-bearing bridges: endpoint owner, q-phase, Toeplitz degree,
  and K33/state-lift debt.

[4] Next proof obligation
  Promote this scaffold to a production certificate generator by:
    (a) replacing the hard-coded pi enclosure with a formally cited backend;
    (b) grouping rows by packet_key and proving shared interval templates;
    (c) emitting Lean/arb-compatible rational interval certificates;
    (d) extending from the selected hard rows to all HYP-2963 packet fibers.
